Mathematical Foundations for Reasoning as Constrained Gradient Flow
A Theoretical Framework — Third Edition

ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

This thesis establishes a unified mathematical framework in which human-like reasoning is formalised as a constrained Riemannian gradient flow on a compact cognitive state manifold. The total energy functional is a weighted sum of five sub‑functionals encoding logical consistency, semantic coherence, topological smoothness on a knowledge graph, predictive uncertainty, and memory retention. Three foundation theorems are proved and validated against the recent arXiv literature: (i) existence and compactness of global minimisers, (ii) exponential stability of strict local minima via Lyapunov analysis, and (iii) the Gödel–Boolean homomorphism that makes gradient‑based optimisation on fuzzy logic a precise instantiation of discrete local search for Boolean satisfiability. The manuscript incorporates all critical amendments recommended in peer review, including the correction of the symbolic energy definition, an explicit product‑manifold construction, a complete analysis of the LogSumExp smoothing and its convergence in Hausdorff distance, the slow‑manifold decomposition of the coupled dynamics, Pareto‑front trade‑off quantification, a projected‑gradient treatment of manifold boundaries, a full Guerra‑interpolation replica analysis of the multi‑term Hamiltonian, explicit bounds on Kramers escape rates with hallucination‑risk control, and a discussion of scalability via hierarchical logical subspaces and metric learning. The framework provides rigorous foundations for neurosymbolic reasoning systems and offers guiding principles for controlling hallucinations in large‑scale language models.

Keywords: Riemannian gradient flow, cognitive manifolds, neurosymbolic reasoning, Gödel logic, energy-based models, metastability, phase transitions, Lyapunov stability, Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz inequality

