2. Literature Review and Related Work

2.1 Geometric Cognition

Ale (2025) formalises cognition as a Riemannian gradient flow on a state manifold. The cognitive state is “a point on a differentiable manifold endowed with a learned Riemannian metric that encodes representational constraints, computational costs and structural relations among cognitive variables”. Dual‑process effects—rapid intuitive responses and slower deliberative reasoning—emerge naturally from metric‑induced anisotropies that generate intrinsic time‑scale separations and geometric phase transitions. Lee & Park (2024) independently propose a Riemannian framework for intelligence and consciousness, where “intelligence elements are conceptualised as tokens embedded in a high‑dimensional space”. The GeoDynamics framework (2026) integrates Riemannian geometry with state‑space neural networks to model brain‑state trajectories on high‑dimensional manifolds.

2.2 Energy‑Based Models and System 2 Reasoning

Gladstone et al. (2025) train Energy‑Based Transformers to assign “an energy (unnormalized probability) value to every input and candidate‑prediction pair, enabling predictions through gradient descent‑based energy minimization until convergence”. This formulation “enables System 2 Thinking to emerge from unsupervised learning, making it modality and problem agnostic”. Qin et al. (2022) demonstrate practical Langevin sampling over energy‑based constraints for text generation via the COLD decoding framework. The broader energy‑based model literature (LeCun et al., 2006) provides the theoretical foundation for gradient‑based inference.

2.3 Neurosymbolic Reasoning and Differentiable Logic

The integration of symbolic reasoning with neural learning has been a persistent challenge. Van Krieken et al. (2022) provide a comprehensive survey of differentiable fuzzy logic operators, noting that “Gödel logic has unique properties” among the family of t‑norm based fuzzy logics. Daniele & van Krieken (2026) prove the critical structural bridge: “Gödel semantics addresses this limitation through a homomorphism that maps its continuous interpretations to Boolean ones, allowing discrete variables to be encoded while maintaining full differentiability”. They further introduce the Gödel Trick, “a stochastic reparameterization technique designed to improve the exploration of the solution space”.

2.4 Stability Theory on Manifolds

Mohammadi et al. (2024) provide a Lyapunov‑based analysis of gradient flow dynamics for low‑rank matrix factorisation, characterising equilibrium points and establishing exponential convergence through cascade decomposition. Chen et al. (2026) propose a unified analytic and variational stability theory showing that “uniform boundedness of these sensitivities is equivalent, up to norm equivalence, to the existence of a Lyapunov‑type energy dissipating along the learning flow.” Hosseini (2015) extends convergence analysis to nonsmooth settings via the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz (KŁ) inequality on Riemannian manifolds, proving that “if the objective function satisfies the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz inequality and its standard model is strict, then the sequence of iterates converges to a singular critical point”.

2.5 Spectral Graph Theory

Kelner et al. (2008) develop “eigenvalue bounds, spectral partitioning, and metrical deformations via flows,” providing rigorous methods for upper‑bounding the second eigenvalue of graph Laplacians. Tishkov (2026) establishes connections between graph Laplacians and geometric structures, demonstrating that spectral information encodes coarse‑grained geometric properties. The algebraic connectivity λ₂ (Fiedler eigenvalue) controls the mixing rate of diffusion processes on graphs through the Cheeger inequality.

2.6 Spin Glasses and Phase Transitions

Dandi, Gamarnik & Zdeborová (2025) prove a phase transition for maximally‑stable local optima in spin glass models. Talagrand (2003) and Panchenko (2013) provide the rigorous mathematical foundation for the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model and replica analysis. Guerra’s interpolation technique provides a rigorous alternative to the replica method, yielding free‑energy expressions in the thermodynamic limit.

2.7 Metastability and Kramers Escape

Bovier et al. (2004) provide “the first rigorous proof of the classical Eyring–Kramers formula in dimension larger than 1”. Their potential‑theoretic approach relates metastable exit times to capacities of suitably constructed sets, enabling precise asymptotics for escape rates from energy basins.

