3. Mathematical Preliminaries

Definition 3.1 (Riemannian manifold). A smooth manifold \mathcal{M} equipped with a Riemannian metric g—a smoothly varying inner product g_p : T_p\mathcal{M} \times T_p\mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R} on each tangent space.

Definition 3.2 (Gradient on a manifold). For f \in C^1(\mathcal{M}), the Riemannian gradient \nabla f(p) \in T_p\mathcal{M} satisfies g_p(\nabla f(p), v) = df_p(v) for all v \in T_p\mathcal{M}.

Definition 3.3 (Gradient flow). The gradient flow of E on (\mathcal{M}, g) is the dynamical system \dot{s}(t) = -\nabla E(s(t)).

Definition 3.4 (Lyapunov function). A C^1 function V : U \to \mathbb{R} on a neighbourhood of an equilibrium s^* is a Lyapunov function if V(s^*) = 0, V(s) > 0 for s \neq s^*, and \dot{V}(s) \leq 0 along trajectories.

Definition 3.5 (Gödel t‑norm). The Gödel t‑norm on [0,1] is x \land_G y = \min(x,y). Its residuum is x \to_G y = y if x > y, and 1 otherwise.

Definition 3.6 (Graph Laplacian). For a weighted graph \mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}, W), the Laplacian is L = D - W, where D is the degree matrix.

Theorem 3.7 (Spectral bounds for graph Laplacians). For the Laplacian L of a connected weighted graph with n vertices,

\lambda_2 \|x - \bar{x}\|^2 \leq x^\top L x \leq \lambda_{\max} \|x\|^2

for all x \in \mathbb{R}^n with \bar{x} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_i x_i, where \lambda_2 is the algebraic connectivity (Fiedler eigenvalue) and \lambda_{\max} is the spectral radius.

Definition 3.8 (Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz inequality). A function E satisfies the KŁ inequality at s^* with exponent \theta \in [0,1) if there exist c > 0 and a neighbourhood U such that for all s \in U,

|E(s) - E(s^*)|^\theta \leq c \|\nabla E(s)\|_g.

Definition 3.9 (Exponential stability). An equilibrium s^* is exponentially stable if there exist C, \alpha > 0 and a neighbourhood U such that \operatorname{dist}(s(t), s^*) \leq C e^{-\alpha t}\operatorname{dist}(s_0, s^*) for all s_0 \in U.

Definition 3.10 (Hausdorff distance). For two subsets A, B of a metric space, the Hausdorff distance is

d_H(A, B) = \max\left\{\sup_{a \in A} \inf_{b \in B} d(a,b), \sup_{b \in B} \inf_{a \in A} d(a,b)\right\}.

