9. Slow–Manifold Decomposition of Coupled Dynamics

The additive energy structure conceals multi‑scale dynamics that can generate slow manifolds. Following Farazmand (2018), we apply geometric singular perturbation theory to the coupled system.

Theorem 9.1 (Slow–Manifold Existence). Suppose the logical variables evolve on a fast time scale \tau = t/\varepsilon while the semantic and entropic variables evolve on the slow time scale t. Assume the fast subsystem has a uniformly hyperbolic attracting critical manifold \mathcal{S}_0 = \{s_{\text{log}} : \nabla_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}}(s) = 0\}. Then for sufficiently small \varepsilon > 0, there exists a slow manifold \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon that is O(\varepsilon)‑close to \mathcal{S}_0 in the C^1 topology, and the reduced dynamics on \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon is the gradient flow of the effective potential E_{\text{eff}} = E_{\text{sem}} + E_{\text{topo}} + E_{\text{ent}} + E_{\text{mem}} restricted to \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon.

Proof. Apply Fenichel’s theorem for normally hyperbolic invariant manifolds. The fast subsystem \dot{s}_{\text{log}} = -\varepsilon^{-1} \nabla_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}} has \mathcal{S}_0 as a critical manifold. The normal hyperbolicity condition follows from the Hessian lower bound \nabla^2_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}} \succeq \lambda I on \mathcal{S}_0. Fenichel’s persistence theorem guarantees the existence of \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon for \varepsilon sufficiently small. Farazmand (2018) demonstrates that “using geometric singular perturbation theory, under certain conditions, the accelerated gradient flow possesses an attracting invariant slow manifold to which the trajectories converge asymptotically” and “to the leading order, the accelerated gradient flow reduced to this slow manifold coincides with the usual gradient descent”. ∎

