10. Pareto‑Front Analysis of Energy Trade‑Offs

The five energy terms embody inherently conflicting objectives. We quantify their trade‑offs via the Pareto front.

Theorem 10.1 (Pareto Optimality). For fixed context \mu_{\text{context}} and fixed logical constraints, the set of Pareto‑optimal states with respect to the vector objective (E_{\text{symb}}, E_{\text{sem}}, E_{\text{topo}}) is a (3-1)-dimensional submanifold \mathcal{P} \subset \mathcal{M}. The weights \lambda_i parameterise a linear scalarisation, and every point on \mathcal{P} is a minimiser of E_{\text{total}} for some choice of \lambda_i > 0.

Proof. This follows from the standard theory of multi‑objective optimisation. By the compactness of \mathcal{M} and continuity of the sub‑functionals, the set of Pareto‑optimal points is non‑empty. Under generic conditions (no two objectives have coincident minimisers), \mathcal{P} has codimension k-1 where k is the number of objectives. The linear scalarisation \sum \lambda_i E_i with \lambda_i > 0 traces the convex hull of the Pareto front.

Corollary 10.2 (Trade‑Off Quantification). Define the pairwise trade‑off rate \tau_{ij} = dE_i / dE_j along the Pareto front. Then \tau_{ij} = -\lambda_j / \lambda_i at the optimal point for weights (\lambda_i, \lambda_j), providing a direct relationship between energy‑weight selection and the trade‑off between conflicting objectives.

