13. Scalability and Hierarchical Decomposition

Theorem 13.1 (Hierarchical Logical Subspaces). Suppose the logical constraints admit a partition into B blocks \mathcal{C}^{(1)}, \dots, \mathcal{C}^{(B)} with a coupling graph \mathcal{G}_{\text{couple}} whose Laplacian has algebraic connectivity \lambda_2^{\text{couple}}. If \lambda_2^{\text{couple}} is small (weak coupling), the gradient flow on the product manifold approximates B parallel flows on the sub‑manifolds with error O(\lambda_2^{\text{couple}}).

Proof. The full logical energy decomposes as E_{\text{symb}} = \sum_{b=1}^B E_{\text{symb}}^{(b)} + E_{\text{couple}}, where the coupling term involves variables from different blocks. When the coupling is weak, E_{\text{couple}} can be treated as a perturbation. Singular perturbation theory (Section 9) yields that the dynamics on the decoupled product manifold is O(\lambda_2^{\text{couple}})‑close to the true dynamics on the full manifold. ∎

