Appendix A Proofs for Theorem I: Existence and Compactness of Global Minimisers

A.1 Statement of the Theorem

Theorem A.1 (Existence and Compactness). Let \mathcal{M} be compact and let E_{\text{total}} : \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R} be continuous. Then E_{\text{total}} attains its global minimum on \mathcal{M}, and the set of global minimisers

\mathcal{M}^* = \{s \in \mathcal{M} : E_{\text{total}}(s) = \min_{s' \in \mathcal{M}} E_{\text{total}}(s')\}

is compact.

A.2 Proof

Step 1 – Continuity of each sub‑functional. We verify that each of the five sub‑functionals is continuous.

Symbolic energy. The Gödel relaxation \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = \max\!\big(\max_{i \in P_k} x_i, \max_{j \in N_k}(1-x_j)\big) is a composition of finitely many \max operations, each of which is continuous from \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}. The squaring function t \mapsto t^2 is continuous. Hence \tilde{C}_k^2 is continuous for each clause k, and the finite linear combination E_{\text{symb}} = \sum_{k=1}^m \alpha_k (1-\tilde{C}_k)^2 (corrected definition, Section 5) is continuous on the hypercube [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}}. The projection \pi_{\text{log}} is continuous, so E_{\text{symb}} \circ \pi_{\text{log}} is continuous on \mathcal{M}.

Semantic energy. The embedding f_{\text{sem}} : \mathbb{S}^{d_{\text{sem}}-1} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{sem}}} is Lipschitz continuous by assumption. The squared Euclidean norm \|\cdot\|^2 is continuous. Hence E_{\text{sem}} is continuous.

Topological energy. The activation map \iota_{\mathcal{G}} is continuous by construction, and the quadratic form x^\top L x is continuous on \mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}. Hence E_{\text{topo}} is continuous.

Entropic energy. Shannon entropy H(p) = -\sum_t p_t \log p_t is continuous on the compact simplex \Delta^{k_{\text{ent}}-1} (extended by continuity at the boundary where p_t = 0 and 0 \log 0 = 0). The projection \pi_{\text{ent}} is continuous, hence E_{\text{ent}} is continuous.

Memory energy. The geodesic distance \operatorname{dist}_g(\cdot, s_0) is continuous on any Riemannian manifold. Squaring preserves continuity. Hence E_{\text{mem}} is continuous.

Step 2 – Compactness of \mathcal{M}. The three factor spaces \mathbb{S}^{d_{\text{sem}}-1}, [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}}, and \Delta^{k_{\text{ent}}-1} are each compact subsets of their respective ambient Euclidean spaces (the sphere is compact by Heine–Borel; the hypercube is a closed bounded subset of \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{log}}}; the simplex is a closed bounded subset of \mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{ent}}}). The product of compact spaces is compact by the Tychonoff theorem. Therefore \mathcal{M} is compact.

Step 3 – Application of the Extreme Value Theorem. Let E^* = \inf_{s \in \mathcal{M}} E_{\text{total}}(s). Because \mathcal{M} is compact and E_{\text{total}} is continuous, the Extreme Value Theorem for continuous functions on compact topological spaces guarantees that the infimum is attained: there exists s^* \in \mathcal{M} such that E_{\text{total}}(s^*) = E^*. Hence the set of global minimisers is non‑empty.

Step 4 – Compactness of \mathcal{M}^*. The singleton \{E^*\} is closed in \mathbb{R}. Since E_{\text{total}} is continuous, the pre‑image \mathcal{M}^* = E_{\text{total}}^{-1}(\{E^*\}) is closed in \mathcal{M}. A closed subset of a compact space is compact.

∎

A.3 Corollary: Coercivity Extension

Corollary A.2. If \mathcal{M} is allowed to be a non‑compact complete Riemannian manifold and E_{\text{total}} is coercive (i.e., \lim_{\operatorname{dist}_g(s,s_0) \to \infty} E_{\text{total}}(s) = \infty), then the conclusion of Theorem A.1 still holds.

Proof. Coercivity implies that any sublevel set \mathcal{L}_c = \{s \in \mathcal{M} : E_{\text{total}}(s) \leq c\} is compact for any c \in \mathbb{R}. Choose c_0 = E_{\text{total}}(s_0). The minimisers must lie in the compact set \mathcal{L}_{c_0}, and the same argument as above applies. ∎

