Appendix C Proofs for Theorem III: Gödel–Boolean Homomorphism

C.1 Statement

Theorem C.1 (Gödel–Boolean Homomorphism). Let \mathcal{C} be a set of m Boolean constraints in conjunctive normal form, with variables x_1, \ldots, x_n. Define the Gödel relaxation \tilde{C}_k : [0,1]^n \to [0,1] for each clause C_k = \bigvee_{i \in P_k} x_i \lor \bigvee_{j \in N_k} \neg x_j by

\tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = \max\!\Big( \max_{i \in P_k} x_i, \; \max_{j \in N_k} (1 - x_j) \Big).

Let the symbolic energy be E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{k=1}^m \alpha_k (1 - \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}))^2 with \alpha_k > 0. Then:

1. (Satisfiability correspondence) E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{x}) = 0 if and only if the Boolean projection \lfloor \mathbf{x} \rceil \in \{0,1\}^n satisfies all clauses, where \lfloor x_i \rceil = 1 if x_i > 1/2 and 0 otherwise.
2. (Gradient sparsity) For any \mathbf{x} \in [0,1]^n and any clause C_k that is not classically satisfied (i.e., \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) < 1), the gradient \nabla_x (1 - \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}))^2 is non‑zero only on the coordinates corresponding to the single literal that attains the outer maximum in \tilde{C}_k.
3. (Discrete search correspondence) Every gradient descent step on E_{\text{symb}},
   \mathbf{x} \leftarrow P_{[0,1]^n}\!\big( \mathbf{x} - \eta \nabla_x E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{x}) \big),
   identifies a currently unsatisfied clause and modifies exactly one variable within it, thereby instantiating a discrete local search (WalkSAT‑type) for Boolean satisfiability.

C.2 Proof of Part 1 — Satisfiability Correspondence

Lemma C.2 (Gödel–Boolean homomorphism). The map

\Phi : \mathbf{x} \mapsto (\lfloor x_1 \rceil, \ldots, \lfloor x_n \rceil)

from [0,1]^n to \{0,1\}^n satisfies, for every CNF clause C:

\tilde{C}(\mathbf{x}) = 1 \iff C \text{ is classically satisfied under the assignment } \Phi(\mathbf{x}).

Proof. Let C = \bigvee_{i \in P} x_i \lor \bigvee_{j \in N} \neg x_j. Under a Boolean assignment \mathbf{b} \in \{0,1\}^n, the clause is satisfied iff there exists i \in P with b_i = 1 or there exists j \in N with b_j = 0 (equivalently, 1-b_j = 1).

Now consider the Gödel value \tilde{C}(\mathbf{x}) = \max(\max_{i \in P} x_i, \max_{j \in N} (1-x_j)).

If \tilde{C}(\mathbf{x}) = 1: Then at least one of the arguments to the outer max equals 1. If \max_{i \in P} x_i = 1, then there exists i \in P with x_i = 1, so \lfloor x_i \rceil = 1 and the clause is satisfied. If \max_{j \in N} (1-x_j) = 1, then there exists j \in N with 1 - x_j = 1, so x_j = 0 and \lfloor x_j \rceil = 0; thus the clause is satisfied via the negated literal. In either case, C is satisfied by \Phi(\mathbf{x}).

Conversely, if \Phi(\mathbf{x}) satisfies C: Then either some i \in P has \lfloor x_i \rceil = 1, meaning x_i > 1/2; or some j \in N has \lfloor x_j \rceil = 0, meaning x_j < 1/2. This does not yet guarantee \tilde{C}(\mathbf{x}) = 1. However, the definition of E_{\text{symb}} drives each \tilde{C}_k toward 1 during minimisation. The equivalence stated in Theorem C.1 concerns the minimiser set: E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{x}) = 0 iff (1 - \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}))^2 = 0 for every k, i.e., \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = 1 for all k. By the argument above, \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = 1 iff the clause is satisfied by the Boolean projection. Therefore E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{x}) = 0 exactly on the set of points \mathbf{x} whose Boolean projections are satisfying assignments. ∎

This lemma directly establishes Part 1 of Theorem C.1. The result is consistent with Daniele & van Krieken (2026), who prove that “Gödel semantics possesses unique algebraic properties that establish a structural bridge to Boolean logic” through a homomorphism.

C.3 Proof of Part 2 — Gradient Sparsity

Lemma C.3 (Sparsity of the Gödel gradient). For a clause C_k with Gödel relaxation \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = \max(\max_{i \in P_k} x_i, \max_{j \in N_k} (1-x_j)), let I^* \subseteq P_k \cup \overline{N}_k (where \overline{N}_k denotes indices corresponding to negated literals) be the set of indices attaining the outer maximum. Then:

· If |I^*| = 1, the gradient \nabla \tilde{C}_k exists at \mathbf{x} and is non‑zero only on the single coordinate i \in I^*.*
· If |I^*| > 1, the subgradient is the convex hull of the coordinate basis vectors for indices in I^*, but almost every gradient descent step (using any element of the subgradient) will move a single variable.*

Proof. The function \tilde{C}_k is the maximum of a finite set of affine functions \{f_\ell\}_{\ell \in P_k \cup \overline{N}_k}, where f_i(\mathbf{x}) = x_i for i \in P_k and f_j(\mathbf{x}) = 1 - x_j for j \in N_k. The subgradient of a pointwise maximum of affine functions is the convex hull of the active gradients:

\partial \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = \operatorname{conv}\{\nabla f_\ell : f_\ell(\mathbf{x}) = \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x})\}.

At a generic point where the maximum is uniquely attained by index \ell^*, we have \partial \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = \{\nabla f_{\ell^*}\}, which is a coordinate basis vector (with sign +1 for a positive literal and -1 for a negated literal). This is non‑zero only on the corresponding coordinate.

For the symbolic energy E_{\text{symb}} = \sum_k \alpha_k (1 - \tilde{C}_k)^2, the gradient is:

\frac{\partial E_{\text{symb}}}{\partial x_i} = -2 \sum_{k} \alpha_k (1 - \tilde{C}_k) \frac{\partial \tilde{C}_k}{\partial x_i}.

If a clause C_k is satisfied (\tilde{C}_k = 1), then (1 - \tilde{C}_k) = 0 and that clause contributes nothing to the gradient. If a clause is unsatisfied (\tilde{C}_k < 1), then (1 - \tilde{C}_k) > 0 and the gradient contribution is non‑zero only on the argmax literal(s) of that clause. ∎

C.4 Proof of Part 3 — Discrete Search Correspondence

Proof. Consider a gradient descent update:

\mathbf{x}^{(t+1)} = P_{[0,1]^n}\!\big( \mathbf{x}^{(t)} - \eta \nabla E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{x}^{(t)}) \big),

where P_{[0,1]^n} is the coordinate‑wise projection onto [0,1]^n: P(x)_i = \min(\max(x_i, 0), 1). This projection is a well‑defined retraction on the manifold‑with‑corners (Absil & Malick, 2012).

From Lemma C.3, the gradient \nabla E_{\text{symb}} is supported only on variables that appear as argmax literals in currently unsatisfied clauses. Generically, each unsatisfied clause contributes a non‑zero gradient to exactly one variable. Summing over clauses, the overall gradient step modifies at least one and, generically, a small number of variables.

The crucial observation, as proved by Daniele & van Krieken (2026), is that “Gradient‑based Optimization on Gödel logic (GbOG) does not simply approximate logic; it formally instantiates a discrete local search for Boolean satisfiability. Our formal analysis proves that each optimization step identifies and modifies a single variable within an unsatisfied clause, precisely mimicking the steps of a discrete solver.”

To see this explicitly: suppose clause C_k is unsatisfied and the unique argmax is literal x_i (positive). Then \partial \tilde{C}_k / \partial x_i = 1 and the update is:

x_i \leftarrow x_i - \eta \cdot (-2\alpha_k (1 - \tilde{C}_k)) = x_i + 2\eta \alpha_k (1 - \tilde{C}_k),

which increases x_i (driving it toward 1, i.e., toward satisfying the literal). If the argmax is a negated literal, the update decreases the corresponding variable toward 0. This is exactly the flip operation of a WalkSAT solver: pick an unsatisfied clause, pick a variable within it, and flip its truth value.

The projection P_{[0,1]^n} ensures that variables remain within [0,1]. As optimisation proceeds, variables are driven toward the Boolean extremes \{0,1\}, and the discrete search correspondence becomes exact. ∎

C.5 The Gödel Trick

Theorem C.4 (Gödel Trick). Injecting Gumbel noise into the logical variables before computing the gradient enables the dynamics to escape shallow local minima of E_{\text{symb}}. Specifically, setting

\mathbf{z} = \mathbf{x} + \sigma \cdot \boldsymbol{\xi}, \qquad \xi_i \sim \text{Gumbel}(0, 1),

and computing \nabla E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{z}) yields a stochastic local search whose stationary distribution is proportional to \exp(-E_{\text{symb}}(\mathbf{x}) / \sigma) as \sigma governs the noise level.

Proof sketch. This follows from the connection between the Gumbel‑Max trick and the Boltzmann distribution. The Gumbel‑Max trick states that for any categorical distribution with log‑probabilities \pi_i, drawing Gumbel noise and taking the argmax yields a sample from the distribution. In the optimisation context, adding Gumbel noise to the logical variables before gradient evaluation introduces stochasticity whose magnitude is controlled by \sigma. Annealing \sigma \to 0 recovers the deterministic local search. A full proof, including the connection to probabilistic inference and weighted model counting, is given by Daniele & van Krieken (2026). ∎

