Appendix E Proofs for Spectral Regularisation

E.1 The Courant–Fischer Characterisation

Theorem E.1 (Courant–Fischer). Let L be a real symmetric n \times n matrix with eigenvalues \lambda_1 \leq \lambda_2 \leq \cdots \leq \lambda_n. Then for each k = 1, \ldots, n,

\lambda_k = \min_{\substack{U \subset \mathbb{R}^n \\ \dim U = k}} \max_{\substack{x \in U \\ x \neq 0}} \frac{x^\top L x}{x^\top x}
       = \max_{\substack{V \subset \mathbb{R}^n \\ \dim V = n-k+1}} \min_{\substack{x \in V \\ x \neq 0}} \frac{x^\top L x}{x^\top x}.

Proof. This is a classical result in linear algebra; see Horn & Johnson (2012, Theorem 4.2.6). ∎

E.2 Spectral Bounds for E_{\text{topo}}

Theorem E.2. For a connected weighted graph \mathcal{G} with Laplacian L,

\lambda_2 \|\iota_{\mathcal{G}}(s) - \bar{\iota}\|^2 \leq E_{\text{topo}}(s) \leq \lambda_{\max} \|\iota_{\mathcal{G}}(s)\|^2,

where \lambda_2 is the algebraic connectivity, \lambda_{\max} is the spectral radius, and \bar{\iota} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}} \iota_{\mathcal{G}}(s)_v.

Proof. The Laplacian L of a connected graph is positive semi‑definite with a single zero eigenvalue \lambda_1 = 0 whose eigenvector is the constant vector \mathbf{1} = (1, \ldots, 1)^\top. Let \mathbf{x} = \iota_{\mathcal{G}}(s) \in \mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}.

Upper bound: By the Courant–Fischer theorem applied to \lambda_{\max},

\lambda_{\max} = \max_{x \neq 0} \frac{x^\top L x}{x^\top x}.

Hence x^\top L x \leq \lambda_{\max} \|x\|^2 for all x \in \mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}.

Lower bound: Let \bar{x} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_v x_v. The projection of x onto the orthogonal complement of \mathbf{1} is x_\perp = x - \bar{x} \mathbf{1}. Since L \mathbf{1} = 0, we have x^\top L x = x_\perp^\top L x_\perp. By the Courant–Fischer theorem applied to the second eigenvalue,

\lambda_2 = \min_{x \perp \mathbf{1}} \frac{x^\top L x}{\|x\|^2}.

Therefore,

E_{\text{topo}}(s) = x^\top L x = x_\perp^\top L x_\perp \geq \lambda_2 \|x_\perp\|^2 = \lambda_2 \|x - \bar{x}\|^2,

which completes the proof. ∎

E.3 Algebraic Connectivity and Mixing

Corollary E.3 (Exponential Mixing on the Graph). If the only dynamics on the vertex activations is given by the gradient flow of E_{\text{topo}}, i.e., \dot{\mathbf{x}} = -L \mathbf{x}, then the deviation from consensus decays exponentially:

\|\mathbf{x}(t) - \bar{\mathbf{x}}(t)\| \leq \|\mathbf{x}(0) - \bar{\mathbf{x}}(0)\| \, e^{-\lambda_2 t},

where \bar{\mathbf{x}}(t) is the mean activation (which is conserved).

Proof. Decompose \mathbf{x}(t) = \bar{\mathbf{x}}(t) \mathbf{1} + \mathbf{x}_\perp(t) with \mathbf{x}_\perp(t) \perp \mathbf{1}. Since L \mathbf{1} = 0, the mean evolves as \dot{\bar{\mathbf{x}}} = -\frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \mathbf{1}^\top L \mathbf{x} = 0, so \bar{\mathbf{x}} is constant. The perpendicular component satisfies \dot{\mathbf{x}}_\perp = -L \mathbf{x}_\perp. Hence

\frac{d}{dt} \|\mathbf{x}_\perp\|^2 = 2 \mathbf{x}_\perp^\top \dot{\mathbf{x}}_\perp = -2 \mathbf{x}_\perp^\top L \mathbf{x}_\perp \leq -2 \lambda_2 \|\mathbf{x}_\perp\|^2.

Grönwall’s inequality yields the claimed bound. ∎

