Appendix G Proofs for Slow‑Manifold Decomposition

G.1 Fenichel’s Theorem

Theorem G.1 (Fenichel, 1979). Consider the fast‑slow system

\begin{aligned}
\varepsilon \dot{x} &= f(x, y, \varepsilon), \\
\dot{y} &= g(x, y, \varepsilon),
\end{aligned}

with (x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_y} and 0 < \varepsilon \ll 1. Assume the critical manifold \mathcal{S}_0 = \{(x, y) : f(x, y, 0) = 0\} is a normally hyperbolic invariant manifold for the limiting fast subsystem. Then for sufficiently small \varepsilon > 0:

1. There exists a locally invariant manifold \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon that is C^k-close to \mathcal{S}_0 (for any finite k).
2. \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon can be written as a graph x = h_\varepsilon(y) with h_\varepsilon smooth.
3. The flow on \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon converges to the slow flow on \mathcal{S}_0 as \varepsilon \to 0.

Proof. See Fenichel (1979) and Jones (1995) for the complete proof in the finite‑dimensional setting. The key condition — normal hyperbolicity — requires that the linearisation D_x f(x, y, 0) has no eigenvalues on the imaginary axis for all (x, y) \in \mathcal{S}_0. ∎

G.2 Application to the Cognitive Gradient Flow

Theorem G.2 (Slow‑Manifold Existence for the Coupled Dynamics). Assume that the logical variables evolve on a fast time scale \tau = t / \varepsilon relative to the semantic and entropic variables. Suppose the fast subsystem

\frac{d s_{\text{log}}}{d\tau} = -\nabla_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}}(s)

has a uniformly attracting critical manifold \mathcal{S}_0 = \{s_{\text{log}} \in [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} : \nabla_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}}(s) = 0\}. Then, for sufficiently small \varepsilon > 0, there exists a slow manifold \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon that is O(\varepsilon)-close to \mathcal{S}_0 in the C^1 topology. The reduced dynamics on \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon is the gradient flow of the effective potential

E_{\text{eff}}(s_{\text{sem}}, s_{\text{ent}}) = E_{\text{sem}} + E_{\text{topo}} + E_{\text{ent}} + E_{\text{mem}}

restricted to \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon.

Proof. We write the coupled gradient flow in fast‑slow form:

\begin{aligned}
\varepsilon \dot{s}_{\text{log}} &= -\nabla_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}}(s), \\
\dot{s}_{\text{sem}} &= -\nabla_{\text{sem}} \big(E_{\text{sem}}(s) + E_{\text{topo}}(s) + E_{\text{ent}}(s) + E_{\text{mem}}(s)\big), \\
\dot{s}_{\text{ent}} &= -\nabla_{\text{ent}} \big(E_{\text{ent}}(s)\big),
\end{aligned}

where we have multiplied the logical equation by \varepsilon and rescaled the remaining equations accordingly. (The topological and memory energies may also involve the logical variables; their contribution enters the slow equations.)

The critical manifold \mathcal{S}_0 is defined by \nabla_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}}(s) = 0. By Theorem 6.2 (exponential stability), local minima of E_{\text{symb}} are exponentially stable for the fast subsystem, with a Hessian lower bound \operatorname{Hess}_{\text{log}} E_{\text{symb}} \succeq \lambda I > 0. This provides normal hyperbolicity: the linearisation of the fast flow has all eigenvalues bounded away from the imaginary axis.

Fenichel’s theorem then guarantees the existence of \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon and the reduction of the slow flow. On \mathcal{S}_\varepsilon, the fast variables are slaved to the slow ones: s_{\text{log}} = h_\varepsilon(s_{\text{sem}}, s_{\text{ent}}). Substituting into the slow equations yields the reduced gradient flow of E_{\text{eff}} with an O(\varepsilon) correction.

The connection between accelerated gradient methods and slow manifolds is further developed by Farazmand (2018), who demonstrates that “using geometric singular perturbation theory, under certain conditions, the accelerated gradient flow possesses an attracting invariant slow manifold to which the trajectories converge asymptotically” and that “to the leading order, the accelerated gradient flow reduced to this slow manifold coincides with the usual gradient descent.” ∎

