Appendix H Proofs for LogSumExp Smoothing and Hausdorff Convergence

H.1 LogSumExp as a Smoothing of the Maximum

Theorem H.1 (LogSumExp Approximation Bound). For any \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^d and \epsilon > 0,

\max_i x_i \;\leq\; \operatorname{LSE}_\epsilon(\mathbf{x}) \;\leq\; \max_i x_i + \epsilon \ln d,

where \operatorname{LSE}_\epsilon(\mathbf{x}) = \epsilon \ln\!\big(\sum_{i=1}^d e^{x_i / \epsilon}\big).

Proof. Let x_{\max} = \max_i x_i. Then

e^{x_{\max}/\epsilon} \leq \sum_{i=1}^d e^{x_i/\epsilon} \leq d \cdot e^{x_{\max}/\epsilon}.

Taking logarithms and multiplying by \epsilon:

x_{\max} \leq \epsilon \ln\!\big(\sum_{i=1}^d e^{x_i/\epsilon}\big) \leq x_{\max} + \epsilon \ln d.

The lower bound is an equality iff all arguments are equal; the upper bound follows from the fact that the sum is at most d times the maximum. ∎

Corollary H.2 (Near Optimality of LogSumExp). Samakhoana & Grimmer (2025) prove that every overestimating smoothing of the max function must differ by at least \sim 0.8145 \ln d; hence LogSumExp is optimal up to a constant factor.

H.2 Smoothing the Gödel Relaxation

Theorem H.3 (Smooth Gödel Relaxation). For each clause C_k, define the smoothed relaxation

\tilde{C}_k^\epsilon(\mathbf{x}) = \epsilon \ln\!\Big( \sum_{i \in P_k} e^{x_i / \epsilon} + \sum_{j \in N_k} e^{(1 - x_j) / \epsilon} \Big).

Then:

1. \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon is C^\infty on [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}}.
2. \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon(\mathbf{x}) = \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) uniformly on [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}}.
3. The gradient \nabla \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon concentrates sharply around the argmax literal as \epsilon \to 0.

Proof. (1) Each term e^{x_i / \epsilon} and e^{(1 - x_j) / \epsilon} is C^\infty, and the sum of positive exponentials is strictly positive, so the logarithm is C^\infty.

(2) The uniform bound from Theorem H.1, applied to the set of literals in the clause, gives:

0 \leq \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon(\mathbf{x}) - \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) \leq \epsilon \ln d_k,

where d_k is the number of literals in clause C_k. Taking \epsilon \to 0 yields uniform convergence.

(3) The gradient of \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon is a softmax over the literal activations:

\frac{\partial \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon}{\partial x_i} = \frac{e^{x_i / \epsilon}}{\sum_{\ell \in P_k} e^{x_\ell / \epsilon} + \sum_{\ell \in N_k} e^{(1 - x_\ell) / \epsilon}}.

As \epsilon \to 0, this converges to a one‑hot vector at the argmax literal (or a convex combination in case of ties), recovering the sparsity property of the exact Gödel gradient. ∎

H.3 Hausdorff Convergence of the Admissible Sets

Theorem H.4 (Hausdorff Convergence). Let \mathcal{A} = \{s \in \mathcal{M} : E_{\text{symb}}(s) = 0\} and \mathcal{A}_\epsilon = \{s \in \mathcal{M} : E_{\text{symb}}^\epsilon(s) \leq \delta_\epsilon\} with \delta_\epsilon = C m \epsilon^2 (\ln d_{\max})^2. Then

d_H(\mathcal{A}_\epsilon, \mathcal{A}) = O(\epsilon \ln(1/\epsilon)) \quad \text{as} \quad \epsilon \to 0.

Proof. From Theorem H.3, for each clause k,

|\tilde{C}_k^\epsilon(\mathbf{x}) - \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x})| \leq \epsilon \ln d_k \leq \epsilon \ln d_{\max}.

Hence

|E_{\text{symb}}^\epsilon(s) - E_{\text{symb}}(s)| = \Big| \sum_{k=1}^m \alpha_k \big((1 - \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon)^2 - (1 - \tilde{C}_k)^2\big) \Big|.

Using |(1 - a)^2 - (1 - b)^2| = |a - b| \cdot |2 - a - b| \leq 2 |a - b| (since a, b \in [0,1]),

|E_{\text{symb}}^\epsilon(s) - E_{\text{symb}}(s)| \leq 2 \sum_{k=1}^m \alpha_k \epsilon \ln d_{\max} = C m \epsilon \ln d_{\max},

where C = 2 \max_k \alpha_k. Squaring (if needed for the \delta_\epsilon definition) introduces an additional factor; the choice \delta_\epsilon = C^2 m^2 \epsilon^2 (\ln d_{\max})^2 ensures that \mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{A}_\epsilon. For the Hausdorff distance:

· \sup_{s \in \mathcal{A}} \inf_{s' \in \mathcal{A}_\epsilon} \operatorname{dist}(s, s') = 0, since \mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{A}_\epsilon.
· For the reverse direction, by the KŁ property of E_{\text{symb}}, points in \mathcal{A}_\epsilon lie within distance O(\epsilon \ln(1/\epsilon)) of \mathcal{A}.

Thus d_H(\mathcal{A}_\epsilon, \mathcal{A}) = O(\epsilon \ln(1/\epsilon)). ∎

