Appendix I Additional Technical Lemmas

I.1 Grönwall’s Inequality (Differential Form)

Lemma I.1. Let u : [0, T] \to \mathbb{R} be absolutely continuous and satisfy

\dot{u}(t) \leq \alpha(t) u(t) + \beta(t)

for almost every t \in [0, T], where \alpha, \beta are integrable. Then

u(t) \leq u(0) \exp\!\Big( \int_0^t \alpha(s) \, ds \Big) + \int_0^t \beta(s) \exp\!\Big( \int_s^t \alpha(r) \, dr \Big) \, ds.

Proof. Standard; see, e.g., Evans (2010, Appendix B). ∎

I.2 Discrete Grönwall Inequality

Lemma I.2. Let \{a_k\}, \{b_k\} be non‑negative sequences and let c \geq 0. If

a_{k+1} \leq (1 + c) a_k + b_k, \qquad k = 0, 1, \ldots,

then for all k \geq 1,

a_k \leq (1 + c)^k a_0 + \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} (1 + c)^{k-1-j} b_j.

Proof. By induction on k. ∎

I.3 Extreme Value Theorem for Compact Spaces

Lemma I.3. Let X be a compact topological space and f : X \to \mathbb{R} a continuous function. Then f attains its maximum and minimum on X.

Proof. The image f(X) is a compact subset of \mathbb{R} (since the continuous image of a compact set is compact). A compact subset of \mathbb{R} is closed and bounded; in particular, it contains its supremum and infimum. ∎

